WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
- Y'all all know him as
one of the funniest people

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.297
in Hollywood. He has done "Knocked Up,"

00:00:04.297 --> 00:00:07.030
"The 40 Year Old Virgin" and "Superbad"

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.890
but he's also a crazy good
talented producer and writer.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.670
His new book is a very funny collection

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.540
of short stories, very funny.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.580
It's called "Year Book"

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.320
and it's out right now,
you gotta check it out.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.019
Give it up for Seth Rogen!

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:22.385
(applause, cheering, upbeat music)

00:00:22.385 --> 00:00:23.837
- [Kelly] Hello! How are you?

00:00:23.837 --> 00:00:24.790
- [Seth] Hi!

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:25.623
How's it goin'?

00:00:25.623 --> 00:00:29.047
- It's goin' all right but
before we get to anything,

00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:32.541
I have, I have something to tell you.

00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:33.437
- [Seth] Yes?

00:00:33.437 --> 00:00:35.060
- Judd Apatow told me that, and

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.320
I want to know if it's
true, he told me that

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.661
it's your fault that people scream my name

00:00:39.661 --> 00:00:42.974
everywhere I go. "Ahhh, Kelly Clarkson!"

00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:43.807
(audience laughs)

00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:46.030
Everywhere I go, I could
literally do anything.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.163
It doesn't matter how many hits,

00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:51.789
what I do in life, I could
save a family from drowning,

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:55.050
fires, I could do anything
and literally the one

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.479
thing people know me from
is "Ahhh, Kelly Clarkson!"

00:00:59.479 --> 00:01:00.844
(laughter)

00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:01.848
- Yep. Um...

00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:03.117
- [Kelly] So is this true?

00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:06.760
- (laughs) Well, I'm
terribly sorry, first of all.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.300
I owe you, I owe you an apology.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.030
- No, it's awesome!

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.360
- It is true, yes!

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.830
That is true. It was, I was a co-producer

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.168
on "The 40 Year Old Virgin,"
I was also in it but

00:01:18.168 --> 00:01:21.960
it was, I was a writer mostly by trade

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.690
at the time so I was
hired to write also, like,

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.067
jokes, we call them "alt jokes" or

00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.570
"alternate jokes" and
we knew Carell was gonna

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.040
be waxed and he wouldn't be able to think

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.689
clearly, probably, so we wanted to just

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:35.697
be able to feed him things to say and

00:01:35.697 --> 00:01:38.910
we don't always like
to only have profanity

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:42.420
so, Judd was like, "You
should write a list of jokes

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.769
that are, like, dirty jokes
and a list of clean jokes."

00:01:46.769 --> 00:01:51.160
And it was hard to write
the clean jokes, and

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.840
I honestly think I was

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.500
sitting on my couch
writing the jokes and you

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.103
came on television.

00:01:58.103 --> 00:01:58.936
- (Kelly laughs)

00:01:58.936 --> 00:02:02.150
- [Seth] And I saw you
and it was Kelly Clarkson

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.390
and in comedy, there's
a thing with like, "kuh"

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.440
sounds, like they say
"Comedy K," like a "K" sound

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.270
is known for being funny
in the comedy world.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.103
- I didn't know that.

00:02:11.103 --> 00:02:11.936
- [Seth] And so,

00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:13.503
I saw your name and it was Kelly Clarkson

00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:16.200
and there it was! And
there's an actual picture of-

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.762
- [Kelly] I'm in between
"Burger Panties" and

00:02:18.762 --> 00:02:21.454
"Throbbing Monkey-Tail."

00:02:21.454 --> 00:02:22.586
- (audience laughs)

00:02:22.586 --> 00:02:24.252
- [Kelly] I like it.
- [Seth] I like how much

00:02:24.252 --> 00:02:25.085
of that was, just has to be completely

00:02:25.085 --> 00:02:26.818
blurred out because it's not (laughs)

00:02:26.818 --> 00:02:29.120
You see, I stuck to
the assignment, though.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.188
Really, of doing one half of clean jokes

00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:32.094
- [Kelly] "Throbbing,"

00:02:32.094 --> 00:02:33.561
- [Seth] and one half dirty jokes.

00:02:33.561 --> 00:02:34.394
- [Kelly] "Throbbing,"

00:02:34.394 --> 00:02:36.095
"Throbbing Monkey-Tail."

00:02:36.095 --> 00:02:38.050
- Yeah, well.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.290
- [Kelly] That's awesome!

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.710
- Thank God people
don't scream that at you

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.090
when you walk around.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.040
- [Kelly] I am just saying

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.920
I loved it so much. I
did, actually. I literally

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.180
was coming into a theater,
how I first heard it

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.780
was in a preview. I was
coming in to a theater

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.000
to watch a different movie
and I was with friends,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.960
we were late because,
you know, I gotta hit up

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.560
concessions for some popcorn
and M&amp;Ms. And so we came in

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.260
a little late and we were,
like, coming in and I was,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.110
like and everyone was,
like and I thought someone

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.290
in the theater yelled
my name. I didn't know

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.770
what just happened 'cause
I was like, "What?"

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.207
And that's how I found
out. They were like,

00:03:04.207 --> 00:03:05.410
"They yelled your name in that trailer."

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:06.857
And I was like, "Why?"

00:03:06.857 --> 00:03:07.690
- (audience laughs)

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.120
- [Kelly] Anyway, and it's literally

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.940
I can be, I can be in
Japan, it doesn't matter

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.517
where the hell I am, Seth. People,

00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:18.744
"'Since You've Been
Gone?' Never heard of it."

00:03:18.744 --> 00:03:19.577
- Nope.

00:03:19.577 --> 00:03:20.410
- [Kelly] But Kelly Clarkson's name

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.300
being yelled during a waxing?
That's what they know.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.916
It's amazing. But, I, speaking of skill,

00:03:25.916 --> 00:03:27.332
you have a lot of skill.

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:28.540
- (laughing)

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.769
I've seen your pottery!

00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:32.435
- Yes! So you're really good at pottery!

00:03:32.435 --> 00:03:33.820
I've seen your pottery on
social media so when did

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:34.900
you start doing that?

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.373
- [Seth] Thank you! This is,

00:03:37.207 --> 00:03:39.490
yeah, those are some
of my vases. Yeah, this

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:41.160
is a vase I made as well.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.115
- [Kelly] That's so cool!

00:03:42.115 --> 00:03:44.960
- I started doing it about two and a half

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.020
years ago, I guess? My wife Lauren, is a

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.440
very talented, incredible
woman and she's always

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.680
done, she's done pottery
since high school, basically,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.761
and she always implored me to try it and,

00:03:56.761 --> 00:04:00.270
she said she thought I
would like it, and we

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.830
finally took a lesson, and we,

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:04.790
I, I loved it!

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.620
I really took to it and we love doing it

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.530
together. It was a really
fun activity for us

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:12.916
to, to do with one another.

00:04:12.916 --> 00:04:13.749
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:04:13.749 --> 00:04:15.770
- [Seth] And, yeah. It's
been great, I love it!

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.220
Thank God, because yeah, I mean, who knows

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.262
how long they'll let me make movies.

00:04:21.262 --> 00:04:24.174
- You have another trade.

00:04:24.174 --> 00:04:25.230
- [Seth] Exactly.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.740
- Well, I have to ask you
this. You're both, you said

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.620
it's an activity you all
do together. Please tell me

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.123
at some point, you've
put on "Unchained Melody"

00:04:31.123 --> 00:04:33.427
and totally relived the "Ghost" moment.

00:04:33.427 --> 00:04:36.870
- We actually, we've,
there are times, never

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.960
that deliberately. We
should. We should do it

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.210
just so when we're asked
that question, we could be

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.997
like, "Yes, we do it all the time."

00:04:42.997 --> 00:04:45.753
- "We are sexy people, yes."
- [Seth] "That's how we bake."

00:04:45.753 --> 00:04:48.360
"That mug you're drinking
from right now was

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.590
made with me shirtless behind Lauren."

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.423
- (audience laughs)

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:52.256
- (Kelly laughs)

00:04:52.256 --> 00:04:53.922
- [Seth] "As we interlinked
through one another's hands."

00:04:53.922 --> 00:04:55.580
- It was made from love.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.010
Well, here's an idea. If
"Unchained Melody" doesn't

00:04:58.010 --> 00:04:59.850
do the trick, you can always
put on "A Moment Like This."

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.410
I seem to be an inspiration
for you, so...(laughs)

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.460
- You are! You're my muse.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.470
- [Kelly] Seth's new book,
"Year Book," is out now.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.742
Um, what's it about?

00:05:07.742 --> 00:05:10.500
- [Seth] It's a collection
of, stories that

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.189
I hope are funny, for
people. That's mostly it.

00:05:13.189 --> 00:05:16.627
True stories, from my
life. Just kinda essays,

00:05:16.627 --> 00:05:21.100
I guess, on subjects as
well that, not so strictly

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.225
kind of narrative chapters but, yeah. It's

00:05:24.225 --> 00:05:27.209
mostly just, it's pure goal is to be funny

00:05:27.209 --> 00:05:29.500
and entertaining, and I'm using my own

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.333
personal experiences to try to write,

00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:35.333
yeah, pieces that people just
laugh at and enjoy, you know?

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.680
- All right, well you talk about meeting

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.150
some of your heroes, so
is it true, that Bob Dylan

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:41.820
almost gave you a harmonica and

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.210
how does 'almost' happen?

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.243
- Yeah, I was at the Grammys once,

00:05:46.243 --> 00:05:49.430
years ago. Only once and
the Grammys is a crazy

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:50.380
award show, compared to

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.940
movie award shows and musicians lead very,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.645
incredible lives. (laughs)

00:05:55.645 --> 00:05:58.062
- (laughing)

00:05:59.561 --> 00:06:03.292
- And as I'm sure you've seen firsthand

00:06:03.292 --> 00:06:04.680
on many occasions,

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.850
if you're a musician, you
can make it that you don't

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:09.133
interact with one human
being you don't want to.

00:06:09.133 --> 00:06:09.966
- (Kelly laughs)

00:06:09.966 --> 00:06:11.253
- [Seth] Which is, like,
pretty crazy, I think.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.546
But anyway, I was at the Grammys and,

00:06:16.546 --> 00:06:19.050
Bob Dylan was there
and he performed and I,

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.765
actually stole a drink
from his dressing room,

00:06:20.765 --> 00:06:23.398
but I personally didn't see him.

00:06:23.398 --> 00:06:27.640
But months, a few months later,
I was at a party and a guy

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.350
I know came up to me and
he told me, "You were

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:31.990
at the Grammys this year."
And I said, "Yes," and he

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.440
goes, "I was there too
with a friend of mine

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.905
who actually looks very much like you

00:06:35.905 --> 00:06:38.754
and while we were at the
Grammys, a security guard

00:06:38.754 --> 00:06:43.543
came up to him and handed him
a harmonica and said that,

00:06:43.543 --> 00:06:46.940
'Bob Dylan is a big fan
of yours on Howard Stern

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.617
and wanted you to have
this.'" And I was like,

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:52.397
"Well, can I have the
harmonica?" And the guy was like,

00:06:52.397 --> 00:06:54.938
"No. My friend just kept
the harmonica." (laughs)

00:06:54.938 --> 00:06:55.771
- (Kelly laughs)

00:06:55.771 --> 00:06:56.604
- (audience laughs)

00:06:56.604 --> 00:06:57.437
- So, and I don't know who
this person is. I don't

00:06:57.437 --> 00:06:59.640
know their name, I honestly
don't even remember

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.303
the friend who was the
mutual person at this point.

00:07:02.303 --> 00:07:03.136
- (Kelly laughs)

00:07:03.136 --> 00:07:03.969
- But if you are watching this and

00:07:03.969 --> 00:07:05.130
have the harmonica, please
just give me the harmonica.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.430
- Well, maybe they're a
musician. They don't like talking

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.226
to people, Seth so you're never
gonna see it again. (laughs)

00:07:11.226 --> 00:07:12.059
- [Seth] Exactly! Maybe they
are completely insulated

00:07:12.059 --> 00:07:13.854
from society and will
never see this in any way,

00:07:13.854 --> 00:07:15.151
shape or form. (laughs)

00:07:15.151 --> 00:07:15.984
- (Kelly laughs)

00:07:15.984 --> 00:07:16.817
- (audience laughs)

00:07:16.817 --> 00:07:19.580
- You need to go to a country
awards show 'cause I did

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.325
grow up with pop award shows
in this industry. Country-

00:07:22.325 --> 00:07:23.158
- [Seth] That is probably very different!

00:07:23.158 --> 00:07:24.710
- Country award shows are
very different. Everyone's

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.840
door is open, they actually
share dressing rooms,

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:29.710
it's a different experience.
Well, you and your

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:32.280
writing partner, Evan
Goldberg, wrote "Superbad."

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.580
Was that scene with the beer
in the laundry detergent

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.389
bottles from real life?

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:38.920
- Um, it was, again,

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.537
another story I reference
in "Year Book" is, uh...

00:07:42.537 --> 00:07:46.790
Yeah, we, we noticed
when we were around maybe

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:50.220
16 that a lot of bars stored kegs,

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:51.850
behind...the bar,

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:55.800
and so one night, and I
don't recommend that anyone

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.466
do this and I am, confessing
to a crime right now.

00:07:59.466 --> 00:08:00.299
- (laughs)

00:08:00.299 --> 00:08:03.150
- [Seth] We went out and stole, a keg

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:07.010
from behind a restaurant,
basically, not knowing

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.400
that you needed like a
pump to operate a keg.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.760
We knew nothing, we were
children and, we notice

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:14.160
there's like a little
pressure ball that kinda keeps

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.090
the beer in so we knocked
it out with a hammer

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:17.750
and then beer just kinda
poured out of it and we

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:19.650
didn't know what to do
so we just kinda filled,

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.970
my mother had a lot of
Tupperware parties, my parents

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.601
were kinda, they didn't
throw anything away.

00:08:24.601 --> 00:08:25.434
- (Kelly laughs)

00:08:25.434 --> 00:08:26.690
- We had a lot of empty yogurt containers,

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.890
detergent containers,
things like that so we kinda

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:31.610
just filled up everything
we could with beer and also,

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.300
we couldn't hide a keg anywhere
so we had to get rid of it.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.168
So, we had to empty the
keg into Tupperware and

00:08:37.168 --> 00:08:41.290
things like that, and then
we drank, lukewarm beer

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:44.040
that had been hidden under
out beds in Tupperware

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.351
and yogurt containers
and detergent bottles.

00:08:46.351 --> 00:08:47.642
- (laughs) Oh, my God.

00:08:47.642 --> 00:08:48.475
- [Seth] For the next

00:08:48.475 --> 00:08:50.960
several weeks,
unrefrigerated, just sitting

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:54.520
in these plastic tubs, and the fact that

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.440
we're still alive is, I guess, a testament

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:57.616
to the immune system.

00:08:57.616 --> 00:09:00.384
- (all laugh)

00:09:00.384 --> 00:09:05.384
- Oh, my God! (laughs) I
have got to read this book!

00:09:06.787 --> 00:09:09.700
You're amazing! Oh my God, it's amazing!

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:11.900
You were destined to be
creative, I'm gonna leave it

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:14.810
at that. So have your
parents read the book?

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.920
I mean, speaking of
illegal, did they read it

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.368
and go, "What the Hell?"

00:09:18.368 --> 00:09:19.610
- (audience laughs)

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.280
- [Seth] Yes. My mother read the book and

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.660
specifically was like, "Why
do you tell so many stories

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:26.784
about doing drugs?"

00:09:26.784 --> 00:09:27.617
- (Kelly laughs)

00:09:27.617 --> 00:09:28.450
- That was

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.490
her biggest note and
concern, and truthfully,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.420
it made me write a whole
part of the book where I

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.440
talk about why I tell so many
stories about doing drugs.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:38.273
- (laughs)

00:09:38.273 --> 00:09:39.250
- [Seth] Because it really
made me think, "Why do I

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.600
do this?" But I don't think
she meant it so literally.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.370
I think she was more like,
"Why do you do this?"

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.645
But it made me think, "Really,
why do I do this?" (laughs)

00:09:46.645 --> 00:09:47.478
- (Kelly laughs)

00:09:47.478 --> 00:09:48.860
- So hopefully the book answers

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.717
that question to some degree as well.

